WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.660
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.120
And today I'm learning
about the Second Amendment

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.750
of the US Constitution,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.380
which states that a well-regulated militia

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.070
being necessary to the
security of a free state,

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.600
the right of the people
to keep and bear arms

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.200
shall not be infringed.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.860
To learn more about the Second Amendment,

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.310
I talked to two experts.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.080
Alan Gura is a lawyer
who successfully argued

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.100
two landmark Second Amendment cases

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.470
before the Supreme Court.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.000
Adam Winkler is a professor at UCLA Law

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.230
and the author of Gunfight:

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.920
The Battle over the Right
to Bear Arms in America.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.500
So, Professor Winkler,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.670
why did the Framers include an amendment

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.090
on the right to keep and bear arms?

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.670
- [Adam] Well, really,
there's been some debate

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.900
over what the meaning of the
right to keep and bear arms is.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.420
But the most common view,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.460
and the view supported by the
United States Supreme Court,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.920
is that the Second Amendment
protects individual liberty,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.010
in particular the right
to keep and bear arms

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.600
for self-defense.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.250
Back in the Founding Era,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.120
the Framers were concerned
about self-defense

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.740
in terms of armed state militias

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.570
made up of common citizens

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.860
who would prevent federal overreach.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.950
So the federal government
wouldn't be able to run

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.100
over the liberties of the people

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.850
because of these armed state militias.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.440
And so the Second Amendment was in part

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.400
about protecting those
armed state militias.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.230
Indeed, the fear behind
all of the Bill of Rights

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.880
was that the Constitution

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.720
gave the new federal
government too much power

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.710
and the federal government
would use that power

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.640
to inhibit liberty.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.750
And so that was the self-defense
of the Founding Era.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:38.980
- [Alan] When we look at the text,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.490
the first thing that
people should focus on

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.730
is the phrase 'the right of the people,'

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:48.220
and in particular the
word 'the' before right,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.640
I think that might be, actually,

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.250
the most important word in the amendment.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.300
Because what it tells us is
that the right of the people,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.560
which is the subject of this amendment,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.440
is a pre-existing right.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.480
And the fact that this
was a pre-existing right

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.920
tells us that the Framers thought

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.690
that there was something
that was out there

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.190
in their legal system

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.860
that they were preserving
against violation,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.860
and in fact that is the case.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.310
In English law,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.490
since well before the Revolution,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.090
it had been settled and determined

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.990
that people had an individual right

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.860
to have arms for self-protection.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.760
And this right was understood

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.290
to enable people to protect themselves

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.350
both against criminal private violence

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:37.030
as well as the right of
people to protect themselves

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.100
against some culture upon their liberties

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.580
by a tyrant or by a misguided king.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.480
This was a right that the Framers

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.210
were well-acquainted with in English law

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.110
and they were very
perturbed that the British,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.770
during the time of the Revolution,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.630
were violating this right.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.180
There were various instances

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.150
of the British disarming Americans

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.640
obviously in an attempt to keep
the colonists from revolting

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.090
and an attempt to prevent
them from resisting the king.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.010
The Battle of Lexington
and Concord, of course,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.180
which started the Revolution,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.640
was nothing but a British march

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.690
upon the armories in those towns

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.940
in an attempt to suppress
the right to arms.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.920
And so what you saw was,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.920
when the time came for a Bill of Rights,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.920
you saw five out of seven states
proposed a Bill of Rights,

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.080
and the only provisions common

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.800
in all the demands for a Bill of Rights

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.190
were the right to religious
freedom and the right to arms.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.840
Now, the Framers were
not necessarily concerned

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.840
about the modern gun control
which is in controversy today.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.060
- [Adam] Today we have a
different kind of self-defense

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.983
we generally think about
with the Second Amendment.

00:03:52.983 --> 00:03:56.390
And that's about individuals
defending themselves

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:57.910
against criminals.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.490
That's a very personal kind of liberty,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.830
defending yourself against someone

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.220
trying to take away your life,
your health, your property,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.540
the life of your loved ones.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.420
And one thing that we need to remember

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.460
is that this kind of change

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.840
in the meaning of a
constitutional amendment over time

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.810
is very common among our
constitutional rights

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.270
as we take the underlying principles

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:21.260
that are embodied in the text

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.416
and we apply them to modern circumstances.

00:04:24.416 --> 00:04:26.530
- [Kim] What about in this early period

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.280
with the Articles of Confederation,

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.980
did state constitutions also
protect the right to bear arms?

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.600
- [Alan] Yes, there
were state constitutions

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.220
that protected the right to bear arms,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.270
most notably the
Pennsylvania Constitution.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.360
Article 13 of the Pennsylvania
Constitution provided,

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.090
quote, that the people
have a right to bear arms

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:50.360
for the defense of
themselves and the state.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.580
In Vermont's Constitution
the following year,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.090
chapter one, article 15
copied that language.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:58.030
And you would see other late 18th century,

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.970
early 19th century constitutions

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.230
keep this language or some version of it.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.830
And what you would notice is

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.080
that this language of course talks

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.730
about the two dimensions
of the right to bear arms.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.480
That people have a right to bear arms

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.050
for the defense of themselves,

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.720
that can only be
interpreted as self-defense.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.680
And also for the defense of the state,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.350
that is they can use those arms
that they individually have

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.930
and gather together and
work to defend the society

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.023
from some force that's attacking them.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.010
- [Kim] This is really fascinating.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.590
So, my background is in US history,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.090
and I know that one of the reasons

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.740
that the Framers decided that
they needed a Constitution

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.260
that would change the
form of the US government

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.270
from what it had been under
the Articles of Confederation

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.770
was Shays' Rebellion,

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:54.770
when a group of armed farmers
from Western Massachusetts

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.320
marched on Springfield.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.400
So they had taken their military past

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.730
as a militia in the Revolutionary War

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.380
and then turned that against
the new US government

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.890
instead of the British government.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.020
Did the Framers have any reservations

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.190
about trying to protect militias

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.230
in light of Shays' Rebellion?

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.268
- [Adam] The Founders did have concerns

00:06:13.268 --> 00:06:16.360
about armed militias,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.670
as exemplified by Shays' Rebellion.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.620
They were worried about
self-proclaimed militias,

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.550
groups of basically armed
uprisings in disguise

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.450
that claimed the mantel of a militia

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:32.840
but were really just trouble-makers
who were taking up arms.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.750
And that was kind of how Shays' Rebellion

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:36.430
was thought of at the time.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.740
And that's why when we think
about the Second Amendment,

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.580
we should focus on that language

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.550
that the Framers were sure to include,

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.330
which is that the militias
must be well-regulated.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.130
So the Second Amendment itself says

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.970
a well-regulated militia being necessary

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.420
for the security of a free state,

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.290
the right of the people
to keep and bear arms

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.450
shall not be infringed.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.343
And so they understood
that there was a militia,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.580
common citizens who took up arms

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.880
to help defend the state and nation.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.300
But they recognized that the
militia must be well-regulated,

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:16.250
disciplined, orderly, subject
to appropriate command.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:21.040
It wasn't a right for any
self-proclaimed group of radicals

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.740
to get together and grab their guns.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.720
It was instead a right to
have an orderly state militia

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.660
which was an important
source of national defense

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.323
at the time of the Founding Era.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:34.810
- [Kim] So, I see that
the Second Amendment

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.010
and the Third Amendment,

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.880
which bans the government
from quartering soldiers

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.220
in people's houses,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.140
both reflect fears about standing armies

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.700
and also the dangers of having

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.570
very strong government military power.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.470
But today we have a very strong
government military power

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.040
and a standing army.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.070
So to what extent are those
concerns still relevant today?

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.130
- [Alan] Well, those concerns
are still relevant today.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:02.820
We're very blessed in America

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.530
that we have a long
tradition of civilian control

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:07.660
over the military,

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:08.830
and, in our culture,

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:13.830
the military has not been
used to suppress the people.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.580
That's not our experience.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:19.020
But it has been the experience
sadly in many other places

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.610
including through today.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.450
And what the Second Amendment
reflects is the notion

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:27.120
that the government should
not have a monopoly on force,

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.120
there has to be some means

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.530
that people might still
retain the ability to act

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:34.580
to defend themselves,

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.060
whether that's against
a private individual,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.560
a felon, a miscreant,

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:43.320
or whether it's against
something more nefarious.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.170
- [Adam] Our understanding

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:46.410
of what's necessary for national defense

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.020
is very different than it
was in the Founding Era

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:52.000
because our society is so much different.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.810
We don't have militias
of ordinary citizens

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:58.380
the way we once did back
in the Founding Era.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:02.420
And we don't have troops
occupying people's homes

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.490
the way the Founders did.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.640
But nonetheless I think the
Second and Third Amendment

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.690
both have principles
embodied in their text

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.100
that remain relevant for today

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.610
and should not be thought
of as dead letters

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:21.060
just because the particular
concerns that gave rise to them

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.930
no longer remain our concerns,

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.410
because the principles they embody

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.520
are more important and more enduring.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:28.970
- [Alan] We have been blessed

00:09:30.456 --> 00:09:33.470
with peaceful transitions of power,

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:35.620
with a vibrant democracy.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.490
Perhaps at least some aspect
of our happy experience

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:43.820
relative to those of our
friends and ancestors in Europe

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.480
is that we do have a culture

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.110
that includes the
widespread of private arms.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:55.260
And that may have a beneficial effect

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.620
in making sure that
everybody plays by the rules

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:02.752
and observes normal political
conventions and norms.

00:10:02.752 --> 00:10:05.840
- [Kim] So, what are some of
the major Supreme Court cases

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.820
that have shaped our understanding
of the Second Amendment?

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.230
- [Alan] The first one was a case called

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:11.860
United States v. Miller

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:14.010
that arose in the 1930s.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.640
In Miller you had a situation

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.110
where an individual was a gangster

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:21.780
and he was caught with
a sawed-off shotgun,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.650
which at the time and still now

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:25.700
does require a special tax stamp

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:28.610
that Mr. Miller did not have,

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.320
and he argued that the Second Amendment

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.550
secured his right to this weapon.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:36.610
The government took the
case to the Supreme Court,

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:40.740
and at the conclusion of the
somewhat confused opinion,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.930
the Supreme Court remanded the case back

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:44.680
to the District Court

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.360
for a determination as to whether or not

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.450
the sawed-off shotgun
was an arm of the type

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:53.690
whose possession is protected
by the Second Amendment.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.190
Subsequently, Mr. Miller was murdered,

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.060
so perhaps he needed a gun
of some kind after all,

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:01.340
but we never got the
answer to that question.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:05.240
So I would read that case,
as other people do, and say,

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:07.480
look, obviously the Supreme Court thought

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:09.650
there was some individual right to arms.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:10.700
If the Court had held

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.386
that there is no individual right to arms,

00:11:12.386 --> 00:11:14.680
it would have been a much simpler opinion,

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.260
they would've just said so

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.020
and that would've been
the end of the matter.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.500
But instead, because
more evidence was needed

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.560
relating to whether or not
this was an arm of the type

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:23.940
that Mr. Miller could posses,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.880
that shows that there was
some right therein issue.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.470
Much more helpful, of course,

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.740
is the case that I argued
in the Supreme Court,

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.020
DC v. Heller,

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:37.020
which was a coherent,
detailed, fully-briefed,

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.633
and exhaustively opined matter

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:45.650
which struck down various
Washington, DC, gun control laws.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:49.440
The main laws at issue in Heller

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.390
were a District of Columbia law

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.720
that banned the possession of all handguns

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.230
by people effectively,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.630
and also a law that prohibited people

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:01.373
from having operative
firearms in their home.

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:04.817
You could have a long
gun or rifle or a shotgun

00:12:04.817 --> 00:12:06.900
in your home in DC

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:10.020
but you could never
render that operational

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:12.850
for use in self-defense inside your home.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.150
Oddly enough, you could
use it in self-defense

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.490
in a place of business,

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:17.750
just not at your home.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.910
- [Adam] The most important
case on the Second Amendment

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.480
is District of Columbia against Heller,

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.910
where the Court held
for the very first time

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:26.900
that the Second Amendment

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.290
protects an individual right to bear arms

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:32.040
and struck down a gun control law.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:33.400
That was a very important case,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.800
a landmark ruling that has led

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.950
to many, many, many court cases

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:40.970
challenging the constitutionality

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.520
of a wide number of gun control laws.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:44.890
The Supreme Court has not

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:47.370
again stepped into the Second Amendment,

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.170
with one exception.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:53.510
The Court in a case called
McDonald v. City of Chicago

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.490
held that the Second
Amendment applied equally

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:57.740
to the federal government

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.480
and to the state and local governments.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.780
One thing to remember about
our constitutional rights

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.010
is that they don't automatically apply

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:08.590
to the state and local governments.

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.520
But the McDonald case held
that the Second Amendment

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.430
and the principles of the Second Amendment

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.400
nonetheless applied to the states as well.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.940
And that's pretty common as well.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.470
Over the course of the 20th century,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:20.900
the Supreme Court has held

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.840
that nearly all of the
provisions of the Bill of Rights

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.530
apply equally to the state
and local governments.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:29.810
- [Kim] How about limits
on the Second Amendment?

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.870
I mean, I couldn't own a tank, right?

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.820
What sort of limits may
the government place

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:37.570
on the right to keep and bear arms?

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.610
- [Alan] Well, the Second
Amendment, like other rights,

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:41.900
has some limitations.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:45.980
The first limitations are, of
course, as with all rights,

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.470
given to us by the scope
of the actual rights.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.823
There are many things that
simply aren't included

00:13:50.823 --> 00:13:52.130
in a particular right.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.640
The right of free speech, for example,

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:57.610
guarantees you the right
to speak in some ways.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.050
But perjuries speech,
terroristic threats or speech,

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.640
extortion is a form of speech,

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.800
none of those are traditionally protected.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.370
And likewise, there are
going to be some arms

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:08.410
that are going to fall

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:11.223
outside of Second Amendment protection.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.020
The Heller case tells us

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.820
how to go about discovering those limits.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:18.710
The amendment, we are told,

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.940
presupposes that people will have arms

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.350
that people would expect to find

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:28.020
in common use for
traditional lawful purposes.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:31.730
And so while a handgun or a rifle

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:36.010
is a type of arm that people
would keep for self-defense,

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:37.890
for hunting, for sport,

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.820
a tank or a bazooka is
usually not something

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.870
that you would wish to access

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:47.280
for some traditional recognized
lawful activity, right?

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.160
I mean, very few people would use a tank

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.450
to defend themselves against
a mugger on the street, right?

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.850
- [Adam] I mean, as a
matter of practicality,

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:58.240
even if we didn't have a Second Amendment,

00:14:58.240 --> 00:14:59.350
there's no possible way

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:02.790
we're going to disarm the
entire civilian population.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.350
So the guns are here to stay.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:08.520
The question is, how can we
exist with civilian armament

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.700
and good and effective gun control?

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:12.310
And I think that history and tradition

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:14.010
can provide some of those answers.

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:16.000
In the same way that we try to understand

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:17.940
what does the text of
the Constitution mean

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.490
and what are the principles
that that text embodies,

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.340
we look to history and tradition,

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:25.360
how have Americans lived
under that Constitution

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.710
over these 200 years.

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:30.060
We should also look to
that history and tradition

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:33.550
in seeing the limits of
those constitutional rights.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.280
And one thing I think is very clear

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:36.650
from the history and tradition

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:38.850
of gun rights and gun regulation

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:43.350
is that there's ample
room to regulate firearms.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:45.840
The Founding Fathers had gun control laws.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.320
We had gun control laws in the Wild West,

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:51.200
even though that was the heart
of America's gun culture.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:54.700
We've had gun control laws
all through American history

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:58.130
and they're part of the
story of the Second Amendment

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.810
as much as the six shooter
and the right to bear arms.

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:04.010
- [Kim] So, we've learned
that the Framers' concerns

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:05.770
over protecting the right to bear arms

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.890
originated in the experiences
of the American Revolution

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:11.200
in which militias played a central role

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.530
in winning independence
for the United States.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:16.130
But a lot has changed
since the Revolution.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:17.720
The US military has grown

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:20.090
and arms have gotten a lot more powerful.

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:21.350
Alan Gura suggests

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:24.200
that the lack of a
government monopoly on force

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.610
is a key component of the
peaceful transitions of power

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.750
that characterize American democracy.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:30.850
Adam Winkler, however,

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:32.440
argues that there's always been

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.910
some form of gun control
in the United States,

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.300
even in the Wild West.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.150
To learn more about the Second Amendment,

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:40.280
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:41.810
Interactive Constitution

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:45.223
and Khan Academy's resources
on US government and politics.

